<<~ kanawai "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/ref/RFC-2119">>



<<~ ahu #/head>>

! Kāpae

A first-class ''tombstone that shadows the layers beneath it'' — a raised marker that defeats fall-through, distinct from //absent// (a missing record that falls through to the next layer down).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/etymology>>

!! Etymology

//Kāpae// (Hawaiian) — to set aside, to put off to one side, to exclude or reject; to brush away. The word carries deliberate placement, not loss: a hand moves the thing aside and holds it there. A kāpae stays where the hand set it; it does not vanish, and another hand may take it back down.

This carries the pattern exactly. A kāpae marker sits ''above'' the record it excludes, casting a shadow down the cascade. The record beneath survives untouched; the shadow only hides it from the surface view. Lower the marker and the record beneath surfaces again, whole.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/core-cut>>

!! The core cut — kāpae shadows, absent falls through

Two ways a title leaves the surface view of a layered cascade (recipe over bags, store stack, membership stack). They read identically at the surface and diverge completely beneath:

| | ''kāpae'' (raised tombstone) | ''absent'' (hard-remove / retract) |
|---|---|---|
| what sits in the layer | a deliberate exclusion marker | nothing — the record dropped entirely |
| effect on lower layers | ''shadows'' them (hides any copy beneath) | ''falls through'' to them (a lower copy surfaces) |
| the gesture that raises it | EVICT · CLEAR · DROP · DELETE | MOVE / promotion source retract |
| reversibility | ''toggleable'' — lower it to re-admit | one-way at this layer; re-add to restore |
| read intent | "exclude this, even if a copy lives below" | "this no longer lives //here//; let the cascade resolve" |

The cut MUST stay sharp. A gesture that ''relocates'' a record (MOVE, promotion `@working → canon`) RETRACTS the source to //absent//, never raises a kāpae — the source falls through and the canonical copy beneath surfaces (residency-model anti-pattern #3). A gesture that ''excludes'' a record (EVICT, CLEAR, DROP) raises a kāpae, never retracts to absent — the shadow defeats any lower-layer re-add. conflating the two re-introduces the OverlayFS / moby#783 resurrection bug: a deleted upper record falls through to a stale lower copy that the operator believed gone.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/toggle>>

!! The refinement — kāpae raises and lowers

A kāpae carries a ''toggle'', not a permanence. The pattern names two deliberate moves, each an operator gesture:

* ''Raise the kāpae'' (put the tombstone //up//) — shadow / evict. The marker goes up and hides the layer beneath. ''Remove-wins under contention:'' a raised kāpae defeats a concurrent lower-layer re-add — the shadow holds even as a peer re-introduces a copy below it. This carries the resurrection-prevention guarantee.
* ''Lower the kāpae'' (take the tombstone //down//) — un-shadow / re-admit. A ''deliberate re-admission'', never a passive fall-through. Lowering the marker reveals the layer beneath as it stood; the operator chooses the moment, and an effect record audits the re-admission.

The asymmetry carries the load. Raising defeats a re-add (remove-wins); lowering takes an explicit gesture (no silent un-shadow). A record never resurrects by accident — it surfaces only when an absent gesture lets the cascade fall through, or when a hand lowers the kāpae that shadowed it.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/bag-cascade-sense>>

!! Sense (a) — the bag-cascade / residency tombstone (WITNESSED)

In the recipe/bag cascade a title may hold residency in N bags; the recipe walks them in priority order to compose the live view (residency-model). A kāpae raised in a ''higher'' bag shadows every lower bag holding the same title — the surface view shows the title gone, even though canonical copies survive beneath.

This already lives in code:

* `MemoryTiddlerStore.tombstone(title)` marks `meta.deleted = true`; the record stays readable via `get()` but drops from `listVisible()` — the raised kāpae (witness: `packages/lararium-tw5/src/memory-store.ts`, "Tombstoned titles disappear from listVisible() but remain readable via get()").
* `MemoryTiddlerStore.remove(title)` drops the record entirely → `get()` returns null = //absent//, falls through to a lower bag (witness: same file, "HARD-remove … distinct from tombstone's kāpae hide").
* The residency action handler routes the two deliberately: `tombstoneIn()` for CLEAR / DROP / DELETE (kāpae hide, shadows lower bags), `removeIn()` for the MOVE source retract (absent, falls through) — witness: `packages/lararium-tw5/src/action-handler.ts` `executeMove` / `executeClear`, carrying the comment "kāpae shadows, absent falls through".
* The conflict surface lists raised kāpae as readable drift state (`listKapaeBags` returns hides — residency-model #conflict-resolution).

the bag-cascade sense stands witnessed in shipped code and named in residency-model anti-pattern #3 (Kāpae resurrection — "First-class `tombstone` op distinct from 'absent'").

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/membership-sense>>

!! Sense (b) — the membership / governance stack (PROPOSED)

The same pattern carries into membership and governance. Model a keeper-cabal or Circle as a ''stack'' whose deletes ride as kāpae — the way a TW5 recipe stacks bags, the membership grammar stacks admissions.

* A member ''evicted'' from a keeper-cabal / Circle raises a ''kāpae on the membership stack'' — a shadow over that member's standing, not a hole the next layer fills. The eviction shadows any lower-layer re-add (a peer that did not see the eviction, or a stale invite beneath).
* ''Remove-wins under partition'' carries the ''siege-safe'' eviction semantics: under a network partition two peers may disagree, and the raised kāpae wins the merge — a member the cabal evicted stays out even as a partitioned peer re-vouches. The shadow defeats the re-add deterministically, so an eviction never silently reverses when the partition heals.
* ''Re-admit = lower the kāpae'' — a deliberate governance gesture (a fresh vouch through the Voice house / Talk Story), never a passive fall-through. The cabal chooses to take the tombstone down; the membership beneath surfaces again, audited.

This rhymes the recipe-as-bag-stack: membership composes as a priority walk over admission layers, and a kāpae shadows the layers below.

''THE MARKER RIDES THE EDGE, NEVER THE THING'' (operator ruling). Every comparable system raises its tombstone over an ENTITY — Matrix over a user, the fediverse over an instance, a CRL over a certificate, KERI over an identifier. An entity holds ONE global standing, so a marker over it demands global agreement: hence delivery, hence consensus, hence the write-race that produced Matrix's state resets and the CRL family's undelivered negatives. ''One failure wearing four costumes.''

Raised over a ''relationship'' the same marker needs none of it. The shadow scopes to one edge, no third party needs the news, the blast radius equals that relation, and ''two edges tombstone concurrently WITHOUT racing'' — because they name different objects. The mutual case the literature leaves open (Keyhive names and defers it; p2panda calls it "still to be explored") then dissolves rather than resolving: A shadows the edge (B→group) while B shadows (A→group), ''both shadows hold, both stand aside'', and remove-wins needs no winner. The place lands in a LEGIBLE state — no authority stands — which ''surfaces a fork'' instead of diverging in silence. Surfacing belongs to the code; deciding which side stays legitimate belongs to the humans (#fork-as-exit, cabal-realm#the-unswept-corner).

the membership sense stands ''built at the edge grain'' (`edge-kapae`). Acts accrete, each keyed by edge, gesture and its own content id, so a raise and a lower written from one causal frontier both survive a merge. Each act cites its causal parents, and the fold reads the ''causal heads'' of an edge: the acts no other act cites. No version number, arrival order or clock ranks one head over another.

''ONLY A FRONTIER OF LOWERS LIFTS THE SHADOW.'' A lower that causally follows every raise takes the marker down. A raise beside a lower with no causal order between them reads //unsettled// and ''leaves the shadow UP'' (remove-wins): a lower never lifts a removal by racing it. The place shows the fork, and a later act that cites both heads settles it by a deliberate hand. A frontier that cites a parent the reader lacks, or holds an act whose id fails its own bytes, proves no lift, so the shadow stays up there too. Forged acts drop before the fold; an act counts only under a hand that holds the edge (#/edge-hands), and only when its id derives from its signed bytes. An edge no act names reads //absent// and carries no shadow.

''THE MARKERS NUMBER FOUR, NOT FIVE.'' `dyadId` derives from exactly (deviceDid, operatorDid), the two ends of a device delegation, so ''the dyad names that very relationship'' and the delegation takes no marker of its own. It takes a consult: the Binding Gate reads the dyad's shadow (#/reach).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/four-edges>>

!! The four marked edges

> ''Operator ruling, approved as presented.'' The membership stack holds four relations that take a marker. A seat in a charter takes none: a seat leaves by a roll (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/nexus-lifecycle">>).

|! edge |! its two ends |! what names it |! the board that carries its acts |
| ''dyad'' | a device and a face | `deviceEdgeId`: the fleet root and the device key | the fleet's edge board, read by one PersonaGroup |
| ''vouch'' | a voucher and a joiner, inside one realm | `vouchEdgeId`: realm, voucher, joiner | the realm's edge board, read by that realm's own members |
| ''member'' | a member and a realm | `memberEdgeId`: realm, member | the realm's edge board, read by that realm's own members |
| ''carriage'' | a presenter (an operator, a place, a hosted leaf) and a Nexus | the presenter's nym at that Nexus | the carriage board (revoke, uncarry) and the antigen (kapae), on the always-carried tier |

A dyad's slot id hashes a veil key that never leaves its device, so the marker names the same relationship by the two ends every device of the fleet reads: the fleet root and the device key. A vouch names one voucher's stake. A member edge names the joiner's whole relation to the realm, whoever vouched: a marker over it holds against a fresh vouch from a hand that never saw the removal. A member who leaves by their own hand signs a departure and raises no kapae (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road#/rulings">>).

''Name the board and its scope before reading it'' (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/registry-filter">>). An edge board holds acts signed by the edge's own holders. It holds no roster, and no read of it may claim a whole set.

* ''The fleet's edge board'' derives its address from the fleet root. It crosses to the face's own devices alone: the sibling channel and same-operator sync. A dyad act never rides a Nexus board, since a dyad shown as set aside tells a Nexus that one device left one person.
* ''The realm's edge board'' and the realm's vouch board derive their addresses from the realm's own id. They cross to a peer that holds standing in that realm, and to nobody else: a removal stays among the parties to the relation it ends (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/registry-filter#/the-private-room">>). They never ride a Nexus board or the public shelf, and no walker and no faceless carrier reads them.
* ''The carriage board and the antigen'' ride the always-carried tier (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/membership-doctrine#/the-invite">>).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/edge-hands>>

!! Who holds an edge ~ two hands, and a lower lifts only its own hand's raises

> ''Operator ruling, approved as presented.'' A vouch edge answers to its voucher AND to a chartered quorum of the realm it crosses, never to one realm key.

Two hands act on an edge. The ''holder'' hand signs with the one key the edge's own id names. On a realm's board that key reads as the key the voucher's worn face presents at the Nexus the realm stands under, the key that signed the vouch (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/cabal-realm#/the-steward-signer">>); on a fleet's board it reads as the fleet root. The ''quorum'' hand signs with the seat quorum of the charter the realm stands under, at the seal epoch the act names. The four chartered classes read at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/seed-and-nexus">>.

> ''Each realm stands under one charter, a seed's or a Nexus's'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-18). A council that meets at one hearth stands under that hearth's seed charter; a council two hearths carry stands under the Nexus those two found fresh beside each household's seed, the operators' relationship a Nexus names (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/seed-and-nexus">>). A vessel's first act in a realm (a vouch, a holder or quorum act, a feed, a face it presents there) names that charter: the charter the act names, else the vessel's primary charter. The vessel's own edge ledger keeps that first word as a pin, and a later act that names another charter refuses `realm-under-another-nexus`, and the climb from a seed's charter to a Nexus's re-pins by a named act, owed; the realm keeps its id across the climb, since its founding act names it. Two replicas of one ledger that each pinned a different charter before they met keep both pins: the realm then stands under none, reads no quorum hand, and every act there refuses `realm-nexus-conflict` until a person settles it. Every reader takes the realm's quorum roster and its lineage roots off THAT charter, wherever the reader keeps it, never off the reader's own primary charter. A realm noted under no charter, or under one the reader holds none of, reads no quorum hand and roots no lineage. So no charter's quorum signs a quorum act on another charter's realm, and a hearth whose primary charter seats its one seat never counts that seat as another charter's realm quorum.

|! edge |! who may raise |! who may lower |! chartered class |
| ''dyad'' | the fleet root that bound the device | the same root | ordinary: one signature |
| ''vouch'' | its voucher | its voucher | revocation: the threshold of the original act, one signature |
| ''vouch'' | the seat quorum | the seat quorum | high-impact |
| ''member'' | the seat quorum | the seat quorum | high-impact; no single key acts |
| ''carriage'' | the seat quorum | the seat quorum | high-impact (kapae and rolls take the quorum) |

* ''Each hand folds its own causal heads, and the shadow stands while either hand's reading stands.'' A lower takes the authority of the raise it lifts: a voucher's lower never lifts a quorum's raise, and a quorum's lower never returns a stake its voucher withdrew.
* ''A quorum act counts under the seated threshold of its own epoch.'' No edge act uses the emergency class. A shadow strikes no seat's signature: a seat leaves by a roll alone.
* ''A door reads authority the way every reader does.'' A key that holds no vouch under the edge it names meets a refusal and writes nothing. No option names a persona index on a realm's board: a person wears the face that acts.
* ''A person signs.'' A raise or a lower changes who holds authority, so a seat never signs one (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/session-contract#/the-hands">>).
* ''Seats on separate vessels sign one act in turn.'' An act's id leaves its signatures out, so every seat signs the same bytes. A seat that cannot meet the threshold alone signs and hands the act on as a partial: the whole act, and its subject in plain keys beside it, so a person reads whom the act sets aside. The next seat's vessel judges the partial against its own charter and its own read of the board, and shows a person what it reads before any seat signs; the confirmation names the act that read showed, and a partial that carries any other act refuses. It refuses a partial whose epoch its charter head has rolled past, or whose parents no longer head the edge's line (//stale//); one whose act already counts on its board (//replay//); and one whose subject or id fails, or that no seat of the head roster signed (//invalid//). A false signature beside a true one adds nothing and removes nothing. Causal position decides each, and no clock. An act short of its threshold counts for nothing and lands on no board.
* ''At two seats the quorum means both.'' Neither founder removes the other by quorum. Each withdraws their own vouches, and the exit runs through a fork (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/cabal-realm#/the-unswept-corner">>).
* ''The dyad marker reaches as far as the root's custody.'' A device that holds the fleet root's key can lift its own shadow.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/rolls>>

!! Rolls carry shadows

> ''Operator ruling, approved as presented.'' A seal roll carries every shadow, and a lift may cite a raise from an earlier epoch through the roll's own anchors.

* ''Citation alone orders acts.'' An act cites its parents by content id, and a parent may root on any epoch. No scalar, clock or arrival order ranks.
* ''The epoch names a roster and ranks nothing.'' A quorum act counts under the roster of the seal epoch it names. A holder act names no epoch, since its hand signs with the one key the edge's id names and holds no roster; a holder act that names one, and a quorum act that names none, read as no act. The charter's own lineage names each rolled epoch's seats, and a hash binds the first epoch's seats.
* ''Every roll writes an edge anchor'' on each realm board standing under the Nexus that rolls (#/edge-hands). The quorum of the epoch the roll opens signs the closed epoch's id, the closed epoch's seated keys and threshold, the causal heads of the quorum acts that counted at the closed epoch, and the anchor that opened it. The closed keys never sign an anchor, since they may stand as the reason for the roll. The anchor follows the carriage board's roll anchor in shape and law (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/membership-doctrine#/the-invite">>).
* ''A two-vault edge anchor crosses per realm.'' `lares nexus seal anchor --edge --out <partial>` opens one signed partial for each realm board under the closed charter, and `--from` judges each against the receiving vault's own board before signature and before landing. A vault with no board for that realm answers `unavailable`; a moved head answers `stale`; an anchor that already counts answers `replay`. The answer names each realm `anchored` or `owed`. The signed image follows `signEdgeRollAnchor`.
* ''The anchors chain from the head down.'' An anchor counts under the roster it opens, and below the head it must sit in the causal past of the anchor that closed that roster's own epoch. A key-set that retired therefore signs no anchor after its roll, exactly as it signs no act.
* ''A quorum act at an earlier epoch counts through the anchors.'' The act must sit in the causal past of a counted anchor that closed its epoch. A retired key-set mints nothing after its roll: no raise and no lift.
* ''A vessel keeps counting a raise it counted at its head.'' A roller anchors the boards it reads. Where no anchor carries a raise, each vessel that counted it while its epoch still stood as that vessel's charter head keeps counting it. A lift never rides that road: it waits for an anchor.
* ''An act that does not count drops.'' A counted act that cites a dropped or missing parent reads //unavailable//, and the shadow stands.
* ''A lift after many rolls'' roots on the head epoch, cites the old raise by id, and counts under the head's roster. The old raise counts through the anchors between.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/accretion>>

!! Acts only accrete ~ the reader keeps what it counted

A board holds documents that any writer may edit, so a hand can delete a raise from the board. ''Every vessel keeps each act and each anchor it has counted, in a ledger of its own that never crosses to another operator.'' A reader folds the board together with its ledger, and it writes a kept act back onto a board that lost it.

* A delete changes no reading at a vessel that once counted the raise, and the board regains the act at that vessel's next read.
* A vouch accretes the same way: a vessel keeps each vouch it verified, so a hand that deletes a withdrawn vouch erases no record of how its joiner entered.
* One act holds one id, and its signatures gather: a second hand's signature joins the first, and a false signature under a true signer's name displaces nothing. A vessel keeps the signatures that verified and returns them to a board copy that lost one.
* A vessel that never read the raise cannot tell a delete from a withheld act. Withholding buys a delay; it proves no lift.
* The ledger holds signed acts and nothing compiled about anyone. No signed board head and no per-writer chain stands beside it: the first would seat a keeper over the board, and the second would list the board's writers.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/reach>>

!! A standing shadow reaches every gate

> ''Operator ruling, approved as presented.'' A standing shadow drives the share verdict, the sync gate and a Keyhive revocation, and the sealed store's key roll follows that revocation.

''One rule serves every gate.'' A shadow stands over an edge that reads //held//, //unsettled//, //unavailable// or //rejected//. Only //withdrawn// lifts it, and //absent// never shadows. A gate that reads a standing shadow answers as though the relation never stood. ''The peer draws Mu'': the same answer a peer with no relation draws, with no refusal that names a cause (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/carry-contract#/kapae-the-antigen">>). The operator's own doors name the reading, a fork included.

|! gate |! dyad |! vouch |! member |! carriage |
| the fleet closure | leaves the fleet | | | |
| the socket sorter, at admission | never sorts same-operator; the device sorts by what else it proves | | | a kapae'd or revoked presenter sorts as nothing |
| the share verdict, per document | the sibling lane and full device sync close | | every lane onto the realm's documents and bags closes for that member | the antigen draws Mu |
| carriage serve | fleet blocks close | | the realm's blocks close for that member | sealed carriage closes |
| the browser's federation floor | the sibling lane closes | | | |
| `lares cabal join` | | the vouch leaves the crossing | the crossing refuses, whatever vouches | |
| lineage standing and the realm's own boards | | that path to a root breaks | no standing; the edge board and the vouch board close for that member | |
| a face-grant seat and the Binding Gate | no seat, no bind; a lease roll skips the dyad | | | |

''The dyad binds at three doors, and each reads the fleet's own board.'' The root's admit door signs no delegation for a device its board shows set aside, so a fresh admit and a lease roll both skip that dyad. The Binding Gate refuses a boot whose own device edge stands shadowed, and the operator's own door names the reading. The join gate answers a summons from such a device with the words an edge that fails its signature draws, and names no cause. The face-grant seat and the sibling lane read the same shadow: a device set aside holds no seat there, whatever record or session it proves.

''A dyad marker and a certificate withdrawal remove one vessel by two acts, and each answers to its own readers.'' The marker sets a device aside inside its fleet: it rides the fleet's edge board, closes the sibling lane and device sync, and moves the PersonaGroup's keys (#/keys). The withdrawal denies that vessel's face-scoped key at one Nexus's gates and rides the face's own presentations (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof#/the-withdrawal-proof">>). A gate reads the withdrawal and never the dyad; a sibling reads both. A marker lowers, and a withdrawal never lifts: after a lift the vessel mints a fresh face-scoped key and takes a fresh certificate. One fact takes one mechanism, so neither act stands in for the other. The device removal door signs the marker and one withdrawal for each face key the device holds a certificate under, and it reports by name each withdrawal it could not sign or keep: a face key that rests cold on another vessel, a vessel that reads no record of the device's certificates, a read or a save that failed. The marker lands first and stands whatever the second act meets.

''Lineage standing reads as a live query'': a member stands in a realm while some path of standing vouches reaches a root and no shadow stands over the member edge. A root names a seat of the charter of the Nexus the realm stands under, read off the reader's own copy of that charter; a key that merely signs a vouch roots nothing. A face's key at that Nexus never sits among the roots, since the Nexus names itself after its seats: a face stands through a vouch a seat signs. A root leaves only by a shadow over its own member edge, and a vessel that holds no charter of that Nexus reads no joiner as standing. A voucher who withdraws leaves the joiner standing wherever another path holds.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/keys>>

!! The key side ~ keys never outrun standing

> ''House reading, marked as such; the operator may overturn it.'' A vessel answers each counted raise once: it tries the seat, hands the roll to its subscribers, and marks the raise in its own ledger. The key shore decides which hand may revoke.

* ''A seat without standing gets revoked.'' A device whose dyad stands shadowed leaves its PersonaGroup. A member leaves the realm's key group when a shadow stands over the member edge, or when the member entered by a vouch and no path of standing vouches reaches a root. An //unavailable// or //rejected// reading closes the gates like any other, and it moves a key only once a raise counts.
* ''Every counted raise owes one roll of the seat it names, whatever acts follow it.'' A vessel that reads a raise and its lift together still revokes the earlier seat. Where standing holds after the lift, the vessel then seats the party afresh. A lift therefore never leaves an earlier key epoch in place at one vessel and a later one at another. Raises read together share one revocation; a raise read after a fresh seat takes its own.
* ''Forward only.'' The removed party keeps what it read. A bag's content key rolls at the revocation, a causal act and never a clock (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road#/the-road">>).
* ''No coordinator.'' Every vessel that counts the raise, and stands under no shadow of its own in that scope, answers it. Each answers once for each raise it counted and remembers that in its own ledger. A revocation that finds the party gone reads as done. Keyhive's removal converges across replicas (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/convergent-mesh#/two-revocation-modes">>).
* ''Revoke authority follows the seating lineage.'' The hand that seated a party revokes it, and so does a hand upstream of that seat. A peer seated beside the party holds no revoke authority, whatever access it holds. Two hands of one lineage that revoke at once land one outcome on every replica. A party seated by two hands leaves only under a hand upstream of both, and the vessel that founded the group stands upstream of every seat. A vessel that holds no hand over a seat says so at its door, keeps the roll, and leaves the seat to the hand that seated it. That raise stays owed there: the vessel tries it again at each settle until it sees the seat leave, and its own gates read the seat as owing its roll until then.
* ''A seat no standing hand can revoke outlives its shadow in the key group.'' The group's own founding vessel never leaves its group: no hand stands upstream of it, and no door asks its own departure (on Keyhive 0.3.0-alpha.1 that departure throws mid-act while the founder seated others, `persona-group-substrate`). A seat whose seating lineage left stands beyond every remaining hand too. Every gate still closes on the shadow. The keys leave such a seat by a fresh group alone.
* ''The roll names the one event a content key follows.'' For each batch of raises a vessel answers, it hands one roll to its subscribers, ahead of any fresh seat: the scope, the edge, the seat where it can name one, the raises, and how the key group answered (//revoked//, //gone//, or //no-authority//). ''A bag's content-key roll takes that event as its one trigger.'' It proceeds on all three answers, and it keys its own work on the scope and each raise. A raise counts as answered only once every standing subscriber took the roll and the vessel saw the seat leave, so a subscriber meets the same roll again after a refusal, and again while a seat stands under another hand. The vessel's daemon island signs each roll it answers, and the vessel keeps a roll beside its mark in its own ledger only under that receipt, so a subscriber that stands later reads every roll it missed and no typed roll silences a raise.
* ''A group's revocation rolls every bag the group reaches; a sentinel document's rolls none.'' A removal travels down to the documents a Keyhive group reaches, and to nothing a document reaches. The PersonaGroup founds as a Keyhive group, so a device revoked from it opens nothing sealed afterwards into a bag delegated to its face, and a device seated afterwards reaches that bag's later content with no re-delegation (`persona-group-roll`, `persona-group-substrate`). A cabal and a realm keep their sentinel document: a bag delegated to one keeps the removed party's leaf, the cap check refuses the party while content sealed afterwards still opens for a party that held the bag's key state, and a re-delegation rolls nothing either. There a bag's content key rolls by its subscriber alone, and the gates' consult carries the removal.
* ''Standing with no seat stays safe, and every door shows it.'' Where a lift stands and no vessel that read it can seat the party, the party holds standing and no key until a fresh crossing.
* ''Mutual removal ends in a surfaced fork.'' Two parties may each set the other aside before either reads the other's raise. No rule here prevents that, and none picks a winner: both signed acts stay, and each party keeps every key and every byte it already held. A vessel that reads a shadow over its own edge performs no key act in that scope, mints no further key epoch there, and shows the fork at every door. A junior hand never revokes its senior, so one party may keep a seat the other cannot reach; it stands aside by its own reading. The relation continues only in a fresh realm with a fresh key epoch, by an explicit fork, unless the realm's charter named a recovery council (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/cabal-realm#/the-unswept-corner">>).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/re-admission>>

!! Re-admission ~ a lift restores standing, never keys

The hand that raised lowers, by an act that cites every raise head, and an effect record audits it. For a realm the quorum reaches that act through Talk Story. ''A lift restores standing alone.'' The seat that follows always stands fresh: a fresh Keyhive membership at a fresh key epoch. Earlier content keys never return by default, and a bag's past opens only where its writers declared that past shareable when they wrote it (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road#/the-road">>).

''A device that returns takes its seat by its own fresh summons.'' The join re-keys each of the group's bags forward and re-seals nothing the group sealed before that seat, since those chunks hold the span the device missed. A holder hands that span over by a separate, explicit act alone. ''An earlier seat leaves first.'' Where a raise still owes its roll and the device still holds the seat that raise names, the join revokes that seat and then seats the device afresh; where its hand holds no authority over that seat, the join refuses, and the vessel that seated the device answers the raise first. A vessel that reads the raise and its lift together revokes the earlier seat and seats the device afresh in the same pass.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/what-the-code-holds>>

!! What the code holds

''The acts and their fold'' (`edge-kapae`, `edge-ledger`, `realm-edges`): two hands, the member edge, the device edge, the edge anchor with its chain, the count across epochs, the ledger with its restore, and lineage standing.

''The doors.'' `lares edge kapae` and `un-kapae` act by an edge id, by `--member` with `--realm`, or by `--device`; `--quorum` signs with the seats this vessel holds of the charter the realm stands under. On a realm's board the holder signs with the worn face's key at that Nexus, and `--as` names a fleet root on a fleet's board alone. `lares cabal face`, `vouch`, `feed` and `join` act as the worn face's key at the realm's Nexus, and `--nexus` names that Nexus at a vessel's first act in a realm; the vessel's edge ledger keeps the realm's Nexus (`noteRealm`, `ledgerRealmNexus`), and every door and reader takes the realm's charter through it (`edge-board`). A crossing names each shadow it met: over a vouch, over a voucher, over the applicant. A seal roll anchors every realm edge board whose quorum acts the rolling vessel keeps, of a realm standing under the Nexus that rolls. `lares edge settle` answers, on the key side, each raise this vessel counts on its fleet's board, and a standing vessel runs the same three steps by itself: each fold of its fleet's edge board sets off one settle, with no timer and no person (`seat-settle`). The vessel's main thread reads the fold, places the island's `seat-duties` verb and awaits its answer on the daemon's document, then keeps the signed rolls in its own ledger. A raise whose seat it saw leave owes nothing at any later fold; one answered with no revoke authority keeps its roll and gets tried again at the next fold. The settle runs on an unlocked vessel that holds the root of the fleet its own device edge names, as the command does, and a vessel under a shadow of its own places nothing. With `--quorum --partial` a vessel that holds fewer seats than an act takes signs its part, and `lares edge cosign` adds another vessel's seats and lands the act once it counts.

''The gates'', each through its own entry point: the socket sorter, the share verdicts, carriage serve, the realm's gate and the lane the realm's boards cross by. One live reading feeds them (`edge-shadow-ring`), and both vessels seat it. It answers closed until its first fold lands, closed again behind a fold that fails, and closed from the moment a board moves until the next fold lands. A vessel at the floor reads its own fleet as unknown, never as trusted: its fleet roots rest sealed, so it sorts every device as set aside, sets proven siblings aside and reads no peer as fleet at the fetch door, while peers on neither lane keep the public shelf. It folds one fleet board even there, the one its own device edge names in the clear, and that fold answers the Binding Gate's ask about its own seat alone. A fold that a board's move overtook publishes nothing; the fold that move set off lands the reading.

''The dyad's doors'' (#/reach). The root's admit door reads a device's standing off the fleet's board itself, and the removal door signs a certificate withdrawal beside the marker (`device-removal`). The join gate, the Binding Gate at boot and the face-grant seat each take that standing as a reading the vessel hands in. The first two run in the daemon island, which asks its vessel through the dyad door (`dyad:ask`, answered `dyad:read`): the vessel folds the fleet its device edge names before the island boots, and the door answers off that fold. The door answers the daemon island alone, and a wiki island's ask draws `aside` by name. No door, no answer inside the asker's patience, a fold that fails and a reading gone dark each read `aside`; an island that holds no door to ask through refuses the bind and the join by name (`requireDyadReader`). Both shores stand the same door, and a browser leaf's reading goes dark the moment its fleet's board moves. A device that returns takes a fresh seat with no re-seal, and an earlier seat leaves first.

''The key side'' (#/keys): the plan (`seat-duties`); the executor on the Keyhive shore, with the roll's subscription (`seat-duty-executor`), which the content-key holder's seat takes on every unlocked node vessel (`content-key-seat`); the fleet's own answer in the daemon island, which counts the signed acts again before it moves a seat (`fleet-seat-duties`, the `seat-duties` verb); the vessel's facts and its kept rolls (`edge-seats`, `edge-ledger`); and the surfaced fork (`edge-fork`). One probe measures the substrate under all of it (`seat-roll-substrate`).

''Who may ask `seat-duties`.'' The verb recounts signed acts under the fleet root and mints key epochs for a face, so it answers ONE asker: its own vessel's main thread. Two roads reach it there: the settle a fold sets off, and the operator's `lares edge settle`, which asks through the store door's `edge-seat-duties` row. That row names one fleet and takes no facts from its caller: it reads the fleet's facts off this vessel's own ledger and board, so the vessel signs only what the vessel itself read, whoever reaches the row. No wiki island, no session island and no peer asks the verb. A caller the island's capability check admits to the store door's row sets off the vessel's own settle for a fleet this vessel holds a root of, and steers nothing in it: neither the facts nor the fleet's choice beyond those the vessel serves. A verb's requester field names whoever the caller says, so the verb reads a proof and never that field: each ask carries the vessel key's signature over its own request id and a digest of the facts it hands in (`seat-duties-ask`), the main thread's one asker makes that signature (`askSeatDuties`), and the verb checks it before it reads anything else. An ask with no proof, a proof under another key, a proof replayed under another request and a proof beside other facts each refuse by name. A vessel at the floor stands no asker: the facts read the sealed roster, and a floor's island holds no veil.

▢ ''OWED'':
* ''One reader under a shared holder.'' One browser island speaks to every attached tab, each tab answers the dyad door from its own read, and the first answer settles the ask. A tab whose read failed costs a refusal and never a grant; routing an ask to one live tab waits on the holder knowing which ports still live.
* ''The withdrawal at a device's removal.'' The door signs a withdrawal for each certificate it gets handed; the face key's custody door stands and signs one through the library, and no production reader yet hands the door the device's certificates or the face's withdrawal history, so a device raise signs the marker alone and says so.
* ''A realm identity at the wire.'' The realm's gate and its board lane ask which identity a peer holds standing under. A vouch names the voucher's and the joiner's face keys at the realm's Nexus, and no socket proves one yet: both answer an honest nothing across operators until a socket proves the face key a vouch names.
* ''A seat-holder's face in its own realm.'' The lineage roots on the charter's seats, and a seat's key signs no vouch through any door: a seat-holder's face stands in a realm only once a seat vouches it by hand.
* ''A realm's key group.'' No production path seats a member in a realm's group, so a member edge moves no key yet; the plan and the executor serve it the day one does.
* ''A fresh group for a seat no hand can revoke'' (#/keys): a standing member founds a fresh PersonaGroup, seats every standing device, and each vessel moves the bags it holds from the old group to the fresh one, a move that rolls their keys. The face's plane names key on the group's id, so the act owes a name the face keeps across groups first; no door founds one. The founding vessel's own seat leaves its keys only by it.
* ''The content-key roll's mint'': the sealed store's subscriber takes each roll a node vessel answers (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store#/what-the-code-holds">>). Its successor version mints only once a production path opens a session signer, and the rolls a vessel kept reach it through the `sealed-rolls` verb, which `lares edge settle` does not call yet.
* ''The explicit hand-over'' of a missed span to a device that returned (#/re-admission).
* ''The fork act'': a fork surfaces at every reading, and no door founds the fresh realm an exit takes.
* A live multi-keeper partition witness.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/law>>

!! Law (Kānāwai)

* A kāpae MUST shadow every layer beneath it in the cascade, hiding any lower copy from the surface view.
* A kāpae MUST stay distinct from absent: a tombstone shadows, a hard-remove falls through. An implementation MUST NOT collapse the two (residency-model anti-pattern #3).
* A relocation gesture (MOVE, promotion source) MUST retract to absent, never raise a kāpae — so the canonical copy beneath surfaces.
* An exclusion gesture (EVICT, CLEAR, DROP, DELETE) MUST raise a kāpae, never retract to absent — so a lower re-add cannot resurrect the excluded record.
* A raised kāpae MUST win remove-wins under contention / partition (the shadow defeats a concurrent lower-layer re-add).
* Lowering a kāpae MUST stay a deliberate gesture and MUST write an effect record; a record MUST NOT un-shadow silently.

<<~/ahu>>

